WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.140
- No contract!

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.100
- [Protester] No peace!

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.858
- [Narrator] For the
first time in 15 years,

00:00:03.858 --> 00:00:06.990
more than 11,000 movie
and television writers

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.693
in Hollywood's Writers
Guild went on strike.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.560
- This is absolutely an existential moment

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.430
for the writing profession.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:18.150
- We want to ensure that
working on "The Handmaid's Tale"

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.383
isn't the last good
job that any of us has.

00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:24.150
- [Narrator] The decision
brought productions to a halt,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.520
and dealt a blow to an
industry that's been rocked

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.290
by the pandemic and the new ways

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.330
people watch movies and Television.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.610
- The industry is saying,
we're still not sure

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.570
where this is going.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.670
We're losing money on these businesses.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.430
This is not the time to
be trying to squeeze us.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.217
- [Narrator] As hundreds
of writers from Los Angeles

00:00:40.217 --> 00:00:42.540
to New York took to the picket lines,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.820
we sat down with the
writers and union members

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.646
at the center of the strike to understand

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:49.566
what led to this moment and what's next.

00:00:49.566 --> 00:00:50.890
- [Protester] What do we want?

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.470
- [Protesters] Fair contracts!

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:53.310
- [Narrator] The strike has pitted

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.620
the Writers Guild of America, the WGA,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.210
against the Alliance of Motion Picture

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.571
and Television Producers, the AMPTP,

00:00:59.571 --> 00:01:01.776
which represents the major studios.

00:01:01.776 --> 00:01:04.054
- We came to the table
with all these issues,

00:01:04.054 --> 00:01:08.310
and what we got back really
felt kind of like gas lighting.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.350
- [Narrator] Yahlin Chang is a showrunner

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.720
on the Hulu series, "The Handmaid's Tale."

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.070
- Blessed be the fruit.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.060
- [Narrator] She's also a member

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.280
of the Writers Guild's
negotiating committee.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.260
She was in the room when negotiations

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.300
with the studios came to a head.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.860
- The companies came back
refusing to engage with us

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.220
on our core existential issues.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.140
- [Narrator] The AMPTP did not respond

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.670
to requests for comment about Chang's

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.050
gas lighting accusations,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.510
but studio executives
say every effort was made

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.900
to engage in meaningful
discussions and bargaining.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.940
Many of the issues dividing the two sides

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.770
are tied to the ways streaming

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.500
has completely transformed the industry.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.140
- As the studios move
towards the streaming model,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.280
it was an arms race.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.840
- [Narrator] Justin
Halpern is a board member

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:52.830
for the Writers Guild.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.480
As well as a showrunner and writer

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.030
for the ABC sitcom, "Abbott Elementary."

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.680
- I've been teaching
here at Abbott Elementary

00:01:58.680 --> 00:01:59.550
for a year now.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.010
The staff here is incredible.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.593
- Everybody was trying to amass
the most amount of content,

00:02:04.593 --> 00:02:07.590
as many shows as they could
possibly kind of pump out

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.952
to compete with all the
other streaming services.

00:02:09.952 --> 00:02:13.860
Then suddenly, the orders of
shows became much smaller.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.650
The amount of weeks that they
were asking writers to work

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.330
also became much smaller.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:19.650
- [Narrator] In a network television show

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.110
like "Abbott Elementary,"
a writer typically has

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.023
enough work to support
themselves for an entire year.

00:02:25.023 --> 00:02:27.383
- Because we make 22 episodes
of "Abbott Elementary,"

00:02:27.383 --> 00:02:31.230
our writers are able to make a good living

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.400
and live in Los Angeles.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.290
We should not be the anomaly.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.090
- [Narrator] But in the
new world of streaming,

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.850
episode orders have
declined to eight or 12.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.320
And unlike network shows,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.900
many stream shows don't go into production

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.358
until most of the scripts
are completely mapped out.

00:02:45.358 --> 00:02:47.070
- There's this thing called mini rooms,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.560
which have taken over Hollywood

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.440
and it's the studios deciding,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.440
hey, we're gonna put
together a room of writers.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.940
But you're not producing the show,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.320
you're just like writing some scripts

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.430
and breaking out the season.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.610
All of the hardest stuff
there is to do in writing.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.320
And because you're not producing,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.090
we're not gonna pay you the producing fee.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.740
We're gonna pay you the minimum,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.160
which is the least amount of
money they can legally pay us.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.290
- [Narrator] In response
to the WGA's concerns,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.420
the studios agreed to
increase minimum rates

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.002
for writers working in those
types of development rooms.

00:03:18.002 --> 00:03:20.652
Still, writers say this
new production model

00:03:20.652 --> 00:03:24.000
has made screenwriting more
like a gig economy job.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.830
- Between seasons one and two,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.230
we had a writer who had to drive an Uber.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.650
Most shows you'll have a writer who gets

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.960
a 10 week gig, or a 12 week gig,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.640
and that could be it for the whole year.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.290
That could be it for 18 months,

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:40.050
and a writer simply can't live on that.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:40.980
- [Narrator] The studios say

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.130
that employment as a writer
has almost nothing in common

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.900
with standard gig jobs.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.560
- The studios would argue that
this is not a gig economy.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.660
For starters, the pension
and health benefits

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.080
are a lot better, and
secondly, you're employed

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.450
on a weekly episodic basis

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.966
and you're getting paid
every week a set wage.

00:03:59.966 --> 00:04:02.130
- [Narrator] The studios
are also confronting

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.750
a rapidly changing business.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.010
Subscriber growth for many

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.651
of the streaming companies has slowed,

00:04:06.651 --> 00:04:10.530
and viewership has declined
for network and cable shows.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.060
- They're always looking for places

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.320
where they can trim costs,

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.200
whether it's reducing staffing
within their own companies,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.720
or finding ways to squeeze every penny

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:19.979
out of a production budget.

00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:21.733
- [Narrator] The last time
the Writers Guild of America

00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:23.898
went on strike against
the Hollywood Studios

00:04:23.898 --> 00:04:25.859
was in November, 2007.

00:04:25.859 --> 00:04:28.159
It lasted for a hundred days.

00:04:28.159 --> 00:04:30.826
(horns honking)

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.537
The strike centered on the way DVD sales

00:04:33.537 --> 00:04:37.140
and video on demand were quickly
replacing rerun syndication

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.150
as a main source of revenue.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.450
The writers wanted their
fair share of those profits.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.460
- We want our fair share!

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.290
We're here because we care!

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:49.495
- The '07-'08 strike was
incredibly successful

00:04:49.495 --> 00:04:51.828
because we won coverage of the internet.

00:04:51.828 --> 00:04:55.380
So for all the shows
that are streaming now,

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.220
it wouldn't be covered by our
union if we had not struck.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.470
- [Narrator] In the moments
before the recent negotiations

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.377
reached a standstill, the writers say

00:05:03.377 --> 00:05:06.060
they weren't the ones to end the talks.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.792
- It was the studios
who came back and said

00:05:08.792 --> 00:05:13.792
if you don't give up on
these countless proposals

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:17.160
that are essential in
core to your negotiation,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.620
then there's no point in talking anymore.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.320
So they were the ones
who walked away, not us.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:23.460
We were there.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:27.600
We were there, we had
pizza, we were waiting.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.280
- [Narrator] The studios
say that they listened

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.220
to the WGA's proposals,
and presented the writers

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.840
with a comprehensive offer.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.550
- The studios have prospered
enormously from our shows.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.510
The budget for writing for
each show has stagnated,

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.790
while show budgets have ballooned

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.020
- [Narrator] The WGA says

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.600
nearly half of Guild Writers
working in television

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.247
are being paid minimum rates.

00:05:50.247 --> 00:05:52.650
To address these concerns, the studios say

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.702
they offered a substantial wage increase,

00:05:54.702 --> 00:05:56.610
and created a higher floor

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.770
for mid-level writers' compensation,

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.677
but the increases didn't
meet the WGA's demands.

00:06:01.677 --> 00:06:03.780
- The WGA is trying to put protections

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:05.730
into our contract for writers

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:09.540
so that we can get a guaranteed
number of weeks of pay

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:14.540
at a minimum that allows us to
live and work in Los Angeles.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.040
- [Narrator] The AMPTP said in a statement

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.300
that its offer included

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.940
generous increases in
compensation for writers,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.650
and that it was willing
to keep negotiating.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:27.390
- The studios have offered
increases in, you know,

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.430
starting wages for writers.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.200
They would tell you we've offered our most

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.331
significant wage increase
in, you know, like 25 years.

00:06:35.331 --> 00:06:37.380
- [Protester] And then there's
the issue of residuals,

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.720
which are like royalty
payments for screenwriters.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.090
Writers are typically
paid less in residuals

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.820
for stream shows compared
to those on broadcast TV.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.750
- The streamers have been
very protective of their data

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:52.050
so we have no idea how
well shows are doing.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:53.646
We have to take their word for it.

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:56.520
We would like some sort of compensation

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.950
that's tied to the success of the show.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.890
- [Narrator] The AMPTP has
provided some counter offers,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.680
but they did not meet the WGA's demands.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.891
- The studio streamers and networks,

00:07:06.891 --> 00:07:11.891
they have offered raises on
residuals and on minimum wages.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.270
- [Narrator] Late night
shows were the first to feel

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:16.980
the ripple effects of the strike,

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.080
with most heading into reruns.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.233
- I also feel very strongly

00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:24.259
that what the writers are
asking for is not unreasonable.

00:07:24.259 --> 00:07:26.793
- [Narrator] Some content
providers are in a better position

00:07:26.793 --> 00:07:29.010
than others to weather the Strike.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:32.100
Streaming services like
Netflix and HBO Max

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.920
all tend to make their
shows far in advance.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.297
But the fall TV season
for broadcast networks

00:07:37.297 --> 00:07:38.880
could be delayed.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.620
- The Writer's Room for
season three of "Abbott"

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:45.414
was supposed to start
May 2nd, and we struck

00:07:45.414 --> 00:07:48.330
and so we are not able to
start our Writer's Room

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.230
and get to work.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.540
- On "The Handmaids
Tale," we were breaking

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.820
and writing our final season.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.720
When the strike hit, we just
all closed down the room.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:07:58.983
It was heartbreaking.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:02.160
- [Narrator] As the standoff continues,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.399
writers throughout the industry

00:08:03.399 --> 00:08:05.970
continue to take to the picket lines.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:09.660
- None of us wanted this
strike, but we are fighting.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.250
- We wanna be writing "Abbott Elementary."

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.756
We all want to be making television shows.

00:08:13.756 --> 00:08:18.756
We just want to be paid fairly
by the studios for doing it.

00:08:18.783 --> 00:08:21.866
(soft pensive music)

